  "date_building_work_completed_under_previous_permission": null,
  "lapsed_date": "02/09/2029",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed rear wraparound extension, by reason of its excessive depth, scale and cumulative form of development, would result in a dominant, bulky and disproportionate addition that would fail to remain subordinate to the host dwelling. The extension would appear incongruous within the row of terraced properties, harming the character and appearance of the host property and the surrounding area. Furthermore, when considered alongside the proposed cycle storage facilities, the development would result in an overdevelopment of the site, significantly reducing the amount of functional and usable outdoor amenity space and eroding the openness of the rear garden. The reduction in garden space would harm the development pattern and the character of the local area. Accordingly, the proposal is contrary to the National Planning Policy Framework 2026, Policy D4 of the London Plan, Policy CP30 of the Enfield Core Strategy, Policy DMD 9, 11 and 37 of the Enfield Development Management Document.\r",
    "The proposed rear extension, by reason of its depth, height and siting along the shared boundary of No. 20 South Road, would appear overbearing and result in an unacceptable loss of outlook and sense of enclosure to that neighbouring property. The development would therefore cause material harm to residential amenity, contrary to the National Planning Policy Framework (2026), Policies D3 and D6 of the London Plan (2021), Policy CP30 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), and Policy DMD8, DMD11 and DMD37 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed development, by reason of the cumulative footprint of the rear extension and cycle storage facilities, would result in an inadequate provision of private outdoor amenity space for future occupiers. The remaining rear garden would be excessively reduced, fragmented and constrained in its layout, such that it would fail to provide a meaningful, functional and usable area for outdoor recreation and amenity purposes corresponding with the level of occupation proposed. Therefore, the development would result in substandard living conditions for future occupiers and is therefore contrary to the National Planning Policy Framework 2026, Policy D6 of the London Plan, Policy CP30 of the Enfield Core Strategy and Policy DMD 9 and DMD 37 of the Enfield Development Management Document"
  ],
